**Date:** 31 October 2026 · **Deciders:** Alejandro Alvarenga (harness-assisted), support desk

## Context

[Decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject) merges two reports into one ticket when
their subject lines match, on the sound logic that duplicates of the same bug usually share a
subject. It says nothing about the reverse case: two *different* bugs can also produce reports
with the same subject line, because people describe symptoms ("wrong day", "date is wrong"), not
causes.

Ticket #4471 was exactly this. 412 reports, one subject-line cluster, marked "known" as a single
parser bug. Run through the desk's own cause logic (`src/lib/kb/causes.ts`), it splits cleanly
into two: `parser` (379 reports) and `hint` (33 reports) — two different components, two
different fixes, merged into one ticket by decision 0001's own rule.

## Decision

Before a big ticket is marked known, run its reports through the desk's cause-splitting tool
(`causes.ts`), not just eyes on the subject line. If it reports more than one cause, file one
ticket per cause, each with its own count — not one ticket for the whole merge.

Filed from this review:
- [issue #1](https://github.com/aealvarengal/mega-s01e03-what-it-shows/issues/1) — the parser
  bug, 379 reports.
- [issue #2](https://github.com/aealvarengal/mega-s01e03-what-it-shows/issues/2) — the
  `booking-v3` hint bug, 33 reports.

See [the full breakdown](/kb/incidents/4471/split).

## Consequences

- Decision 0001 still stands for genuine duplicates. This adds a check in the other direction:
  same subject is necessary for a merge, not sufficient to assume one cause.
- A ticket this size needs its cause distribution checked once, before it's marked known — not
  reopened and resplit later once someone notices the fix didn't close all of it.
- `causes.ts` already existed to do this check. Running it on every big ticket going forward is
  a habit, not new tooling.

